Z||000000||Cheever2||05-21-2001||11:07 AM||||Maybe I didn't do such a great job after all.  You know the parts above your head that are "indented" - when the board is off and laying upside-down, the two bowl-shaped areas (no sunroof) - well, once you have the adhesive on both the material and the board then lay the material on the board, you have to push the material into these bowls.  Problem is, the material around that was already stuck and kept me from getting it all the way into there.  It was far enough, or so I thought, until coming home from the show yesterday I'm feeling the headliner on my head more with every passing mile!<P>See what you think of this for a repair - since I can't just push the material into those recessed shapes because there's not enough "give" to the already-stuck material around them, I will have to cut into it, leaving empty spaces in the middle of each (directly over the driver & passenger's heads).  What if I "inlay" a contrasting square of black headliner material there?  I think it would look good since the dash is darker than the grey carpet and seats.  This would add a little custom cosmetic touch I hope.  It may need a little molding strip of some type around the edge to hide the seams between the black and the grey, but that should be available somewhere.  I believe this can be done without removing the board again.  Your thoughts?<P>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Topknot||05-21-2001||11:26 AM||||Very interesting idea...To my knowledge I haven't seen any custom headliners like what your describing.<P>One thing you could do is when you cut out the two overhead areas leave a couple inches of extra fabric so that when you go to inlay the other fabric you can make a reverse seam and then fold this up and inside the backside of the headliner thus giving your two fabrics a nice seam where they meet.<P>Just my 3 cents worth...of cource you could always custom make a few fabriticated hard headliner covers with a little fiberglass type work like that that is done for carbon fiber making.  Saw a few links about doing this at home in another posting.<P>Topknot =]<BR>||||reg||1
